{"subjectId":"python","id":"regex","title":"Regular Expressions (re)","group":"Strings & Numbers","order":14,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A regular expression is a tiny pattern language for matching text. Python's <code>re</code> module finds, extracts, validates, splits and rewrites strings with it.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Rule #1</div><p>Always write patterns as <strong>raw strings</strong> — <code>r'\\d+'</code> — so Python doesn't interpret the backslashes before <code>re</code> sees them.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>The five ways to match</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Returns</th><th>Matches where?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>re.match(p, s)</code></td><td><code>Match</code> or <code>None</code></td><td>Only at the <strong>start</strong> of <code>s</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>re.search(p, s)</code></td><td><code>Match</code> or <code>None</code></td><td>The <strong>first</strong> place anywhere in <code>s</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>re.fullmatch(p, s)</code></td><td><code>Match</code> or <code>None</code></td><td>The <strong>whole</strong> string — best for validation</td></tr><tr><td><code>re.findall(p, s)</code></td><td><code>list</code> of strings (or tuples of groups)</td><td>Every non-overlapping match</td></tr><tr><td><code>re.finditer(p, s)</code></td><td>Iterator of <code>Match</code> objects</td><td>Every match, lazily, with positions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import re\ns = \"Order 66 shipped on 2026-09-29\"\nprint(re.match(r\"\\d+\", s))                    # None   ← s doesn't start with a digit\nm = re.search(r\"\\d+\", s)\nprint(m.group(), m.start(), m.end(), m.span())   # 66 6 8 (6, 8)\nprint(re.findall(r\"\\d+\", s))                  # ['66', '2026', '09', '29']\nprint(bool(re.fullmatch(r\"\\d{4}\", \"2026\")), bool(re.fullmatch(r\"\\d{4}\", \"20261\")))   # True False\nprint([m.start() for m in re.finditer(r\"\\d+\", s)])   # [6, 20, 25, 28]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>re.match</code> only anchors at the start, not the end: <code>re.match(r'\\d+', '12abc')</code> succeeds. For validation use <code>re.fullmatch</code> (or <code>^…$</code> with <code>search</code>).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-details\"></i>Pattern syntax cheat sheet</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Matches</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>.</code></td><td>Any character except newline (any at all with <code>re.DOTALL</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>\\d</code> / <code>\\D</code></td><td>Digit / non-digit (Unicode digits by default)</td></tr><tr><td><code>\\w</code> / <code>\\W</code></td><td>Word char <code>[A-Za-z0-9_]</code> + Unicode letters / anything else</td></tr><tr><td><code>\\s</code> / <code>\\S</code></td><td>Whitespace / non-whitespace</td></tr><tr><td><code>[abc]</code>, <code>[a-z]</code>, <code>[^0-9]</code></td><td>Character class, range, negated class</td></tr><tr><td><code>^</code> / <code>$</code></td><td>Start / end of string (of each line with <code>re.MULTILINE</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>\\A</code> / <code>\\Z</code></td><td>Start / end of string, always</td></tr><tr><td><code>\\b</code> / <code>\\B</code></td><td>Word boundary / not a boundary</td></tr><tr><td><code>a|b</code></td><td>Alternation: <code>a</code> or <code>b</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>\\.</code>, <code>\\*</code>, <code>\\(</code></td><td>Escaped literal metacharacters (or use <code>re.escape(text)</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Quantifier</th><th>Means</th><th>Lazy version</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>*</code></td><td>0 or more</td><td><code>*?</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>+</code></td><td>1 or more</td><td><code>+?</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>?</code></td><td>0 or 1 (optional)</td><td><code>??</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>{n}</code></td><td>Exactly n</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>{n,m}</code></td><td>Between n and m</td><td><code>{n,m}?</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>{n,}</code></td><td>n or more</td><td><code>{n,}?</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import re\ntext = \"cat catalog concat Cat\"\nprint(re.findall(r\"cat\", text))              # ['cat', 'cat', 'cat']\nprint(re.findall(r\"\\bcat\\b\", text))          # ['cat']\nprint(re.findall(r\"\\bcat\\b\", text, re.I))    # ['cat', 'Cat']\nprint(re.findall(r\"colou?r\", \"color colour\"))    # ['color', 'colour']\nprint(re.findall(r\"[A-Z][a-z]+\", \"Asha met Ravi in Pune\"))   # ['Asha', 'Ravi', 'Pune']\nprint(re.findall(r\"\\d{2,3}\", \"1 12 123 1234\"))  # ['12', '123', '123']\nprint(re.escape(\"1+1=2?\"))                   # 1\\+1=2\\?</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i>Groups: capture, name, skip</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import re\nm = re.search(r\"(\\d{4})-(\\d{2})-(\\d{2})\", \"due 2026-09-29!\")\nprint(m.group(0), m.group(1), m.groups())    # 2026-09-29 2026 ('2026', '09', '29')\n\nm = re.search(r\"(?P&lt;user&gt;[\\w.]+)@(?P&lt;domain&gt;[\\w.]+)\", \"mail asha.k@lvn.io now\")\nprint(m[\"user\"], m.group(\"domain\"))          # asha.k lvn.io\nprint(m.groupdict())                         # {'user': 'asha.k', 'domain': 'lvn.io'}\n\nprint(re.findall(r\"(\\w+)=(\\d+)\", \"a=1, b=22\"))       # [('a', '1'), ('b', '22')]\nprint(re.findall(r\"(?:ab)+\", \"ababab xab\"))          # ['ababab', 'ab']\nprint(re.findall(r\"(ab)+\", \"ababab xab\"))            # ['ab', 'ab']   ← only the last repetition</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Syntax</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>(…)</code></td><td>Capturing group — <code>m.group(1)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(?P&lt;name&gt;…)</code></td><td>Named group — <code>m['name']</code>, <code>m.groupdict()</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(?:…)</code></td><td>Non-capturing group — groups for <code>|</code> or quantifiers without capturing</td></tr><tr><td><code>\\1</code>, <code>(?P=name)</code></td><td>Backreference to what a group matched</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>With groups in the pattern, <code>findall</code> returns the <strong>groups</strong>, not the whole match. Use <code>(?:…)</code> for grouping you don't want captured, or <code>finditer</code> + <code>m.group(0)</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-back\"></i>Backreferences</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import re\nprint(re.findall(r\"\\b(\\w+) \\1\\b\", \"this is is a test test\"))   # ['is', 'test']\nprint(bool(re.fullmatch(r\"(['\\\"]).*\\1\", \"'quoted'\")))           # True\nprint(re.sub(r\"\\b(\\w+) \\1\\b\", r\"\\1\", \"the the cat sat sat\"))    # the cat sat</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-replace\"></i>Substitute &amp; split</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import re\nprint(re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", \"too    many\\n spaces\"))                 # too many spaces\nprint(re.sub(r\"(\\d{4})-(\\d{2})-(\\d{2})\", r\"\\3/\\2/\\1\", \"2026-09-29\"))   # 29/09/2026\nprint(re.sub(r\"(?P&lt;y&gt;\\d{4})\", r\"&lt;\\g&lt;y&gt;&gt;\", \"in 2026\"))                # in &lt;2026&gt;\nprint(re.sub(r\"\\d+\", lambda m: str(int(m.group()) * 2), \"3 apples, 10 pears\"))   # 6 apples, 20 pears\nprint(re.subn(r\"a\", \"4\", \"banana\"))                                  # ('b4n4n4', 3)\nprint(re.split(r\"[,;]\\s*\", \"a, b;c;  d\"))                           # ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd']\nprint(re.split(r\"([,;])\", \"a,b;c\"))                                  # ['a', ',', 'b', ';', 'c']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>re.sub</code> accepts a <strong>function</strong> as the replacement: it receives each <code>Match</code> and returns the new text — perfect for computed replacements.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-flag\"></i>Flags</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Inline</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>re.IGNORECASE</code> / <code>re.I</code></td><td><code>(?i)</code></td><td>Case-insensitive</td></tr><tr><td><code>re.MULTILINE</code> / <code>re.M</code></td><td><code>(?m)</code></td><td><code>^</code> and <code>$</code> match at each line</td></tr><tr><td><code>re.DOTALL</code> / <code>re.S</code></td><td><code>(?s)</code></td><td><code>.</code> also matches newline</td></tr><tr><td><code>re.VERBOSE</code> / <code>re.X</code></td><td><code>(?x)</code></td><td>Ignore whitespace, allow <code>#</code> comments in the pattern</td></tr><tr><td><code>re.ASCII</code> / <code>re.A</code></td><td><code>(?a)</code></td><td><code>\\w</code>, <code>\\d</code>, <code>\\s</code> match ASCII only</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import re\nlog = \"ERROR disk\\nINFO ok\\nERROR net\"\nprint(re.findall(r\"^ERROR (\\w+)\", log))                 # ['disk']\nprint(re.findall(r\"^ERROR (\\w+)\", log, re.M))           # ['disk', 'net']\nprint(re.search(r\"disk.INFO\", log))                     # None\nprint(re.search(r\"disk.INFO\", log, re.S).group() == \"disk\\nINFO\")   # True\nphone = re.compile(r\"\"\"\n    (\\d{3})     # area code\n    [-.\\s]?     # optional separator\n    (\\d{4})     # number\n\"\"\", re.VERBOSE)\nprint(phone.search(\"call 080-4455\").groups())           # ('080', '4455')</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-maximize\"></i>Greedy vs lazy</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import re\nhtml = \"&lt;b&gt;bold&lt;/b&gt; and &lt;i&gt;italic&lt;/i&gt;\"\nprint(re.findall(r\"&lt;.+&gt;\", html))     # ['&lt;b&gt;bold&lt;/b&gt; and &lt;i&gt;italic&lt;/i&gt;']   ← greedy: as much as possible\nprint(re.findall(r\"&lt;.+?&gt;\", html))    # ['&lt;b&gt;', '&lt;/b&gt;', '&lt;i&gt;', '&lt;/i&gt;']     ← lazy: as little as possible\nprint(re.findall(r\"&lt;[^&gt;]+&gt;\", html))  # ['&lt;b&gt;', '&lt;/b&gt;', '&lt;i&gt;', '&lt;/i&gt;']     ← negated class: fastest &amp; clearest</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-eye\"></i>Lookahead &amp; lookbehind</h3>\n<p>Lookarounds check what is next to the current position <strong>without consuming it</strong> — they are zero-width, so they don't appear in the match.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Syntax</th><th>Name</th><th>Asserts that…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>X(?=Y)</code></td><td>Positive lookahead</td><td><code>Y</code> follows</td></tr><tr><td><code>X(?!Y)</code></td><td>Negative lookahead</td><td><code>Y</code> does not follow</td></tr><tr><td><code>(?&lt;=Y)X</code></td><td>Positive lookbehind</td><td><code>Y</code> precedes (fixed width)</td></tr><tr><td><code>(?&lt;!Y)X</code></td><td>Negative lookbehind</td><td><code>Y</code> does not precede</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import re\nprint(re.findall(r\"\\d+(?=%)\", \"5% of 200 is 10\"))        # ['5']\nprint(re.findall(r\"(?&lt;=₹)\\d+\", \"₹250 or $30\"))           # ['250']\nprint(re.findall(r\"\\b\\d+\\b(?!%)\", \"5% of 200 is 10\"))    # ['200', '10']\nprint(re.sub(r\"\\B(?=(\\d{3})+(?!\\d))\", \",\", \"1234567\"))   # 1,234,567\n\nstrong = re.compile(r\"(?=.*[a-z])(?=.*[A-Z])(?=.*\\d).{8,}\")\nprint(bool(strong.fullmatch(\"Passw0rdX\")), bool(strong.fullmatch(\"password1\")))   # True False</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Compile &amp; reuse</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import re\nDATE = re.compile(r\"(?P&lt;y&gt;\\d{4})-(?P&lt;m&gt;\\d{2})-(?P&lt;d&gt;\\d{2})\")\nrows = [\"2026-01-05 login\", \"bad row\", \"2026-02-11 logout\"]\ndates = [m[\"m\"] for r in rows if (m := DATE.search(r))]\nprint(dates)                                  # ['01', '02']\nprint(DATE.pattern[:6], DATE.groups)          # (?P&lt;y&gt; 3</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>re</code> caches recently used patterns, so <code>re.compile</code> is mostly about <strong>readability and reuse</strong> — a named, documented constant at module level.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tools\"></i>Practical recipes</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import re\nEMAIL = re.compile(r\"[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+(\\.[\\w-]+)+\")   # good enough for forms, not RFC 5322\nprint(bool(EMAIL.fullmatch(\"a.b+tag@mail.co.in\")), bool(EMAIL.fullmatch(\"a@b\")))   # True False\n\ntokens = re.findall(r\"\\d+\\.?\\d*|[-+*/()]\", \"3.5*(2+10)/4\")\nprint(tokens)          # ['3.5', '*', '(', '2', '+', '10', ')', '/', '4']\n\ndef snake(name):\n    return re.sub(r\"(?&lt;!^)(?=[A-Z])\", \"_\", name).lower()\nprint(snake(\"parseHTTPResponseCode\"))       # parse_h_t_t_p_response_code\nprint(re.sub(r\"([a-z0-9])([A-Z])\", r\"\\1_\\2\", \"userIdCount\").lower())   # user_id_count\n\nprint(re.findall(r\"#(\\w+)\", \"loving #python and #regex!\"))   # ['python', 'regex']</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Performance &amp; when not to use regex</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">ReDoS</div><p>Nested quantifiers like <code>(a+)+$</code> or <code>(\\w+\\s?)*$</code> can cause <strong>catastrophic backtracking</strong>: on a near-miss input the engine tries exponentially many paths and hangs (a ReDoS vulnerability). Avoid ambiguous nested repetition; prefer specific classes like <code>[^,]*</code>. Python 3.11+ also supports atomic groups <code>(?&gt;…)</code> and possessive quantifiers <code>*+</code> to prevent backtracking.</p></div>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Use plain string methods when they suffice: <code>in</code>, <code>startswith</code>, <code>split</code>, <code>partition</code>, <code>str.isdigit</code> are faster and clearer.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Parsing HTML, JSON, CSV or email addresses fully with regex — use <code>html.parser</code>/BeautifulSoup, <code>json</code>, <code>csv</code>, <code>email.utils</code>.</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview questions</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>re.match vs re.search vs re.fullmatch?</summary><div><p><code>match</code> tries only at position 0; <code>search</code> scans for the first match anywhere; <code>fullmatch</code> requires the pattern to match the entire string. Use <code>fullmatch</code> for validation.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What's the difference between greedy and lazy quantifiers?</summary><div><p>Greedy (<code>*</code>, <code>+</code>, <code>{n,m}</code>) consume as much as possible and backtrack; lazy (<code>*?</code>, <code>+?</code>) consume as little as possible. <code>&lt;.+&gt;</code> over <code>&lt;b&gt;x&lt;/b&gt;</code> matches the whole thing, <code>&lt;.+?&gt;</code> matches <code>&lt;b&gt;</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why use raw strings for regex?</summary><div><p>In a normal string <code>'\\b'</code> is a backspace character and <code>'\\1'</code> is chr(1); Python would process the escapes before <code>re</code> sees them. <code>r'\\b'</code> passes the backslash through literally.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Extract all numbers (including decimals and negatives) from a string.</summary><div><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import re\nprint(re.findall(r\"-?\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?\", \"t=-3.5, x=42, y=0.25\"))   # ['-3.5', '42', '0.25']</code></pre></figure><p>The non-capturing group keeps <code>findall</code> returning whole matches.</p></div></details></section>"}